l_title": "Phys.Rev.Lett."}, "misc": ["The vanishing of the first order decoherence time is well known in the exact Hamiltonian framework:", "Piza and H. E. Borges", "However, our calculation of this effect in the OSR seems to be new, as well as the observation that this is related to the finiteness of the system part of the system-bath interaction"], "authors": [{"full_name": "Kim, J.I."}, {"full_name": "Nemes, M.C."}, {"full_name": "de Toledo, A.F.D."}]}, "raw_refs": [{"schema": "text", "value": "[31] The vanishing of the first order decoherence time is well known in the exact Hamiltonian framework: J. I. Kim, M. C. Nemes, A. F. D. de Toledo Piza and H. E. Borges, Phys. Rev. Lett. 77, 207 (1996). However, our calculation of this effect in the OSR seems to be new, as well as the observation that this is related to the finiteness of the system part of the system-bath interaction."}]}, {"reference": {"imprint": {"publisher": "LANL"}, "arxiv_eprint": "quant-ph/9704030", "label": "32", "publication_info": {"journal_volume": "55", "artid": "114", "year": 1997, "page_start": "114", "journal_title": "Phys.Rev.A"}, "misc": ["preprint"], "authors": [{"full_name": "Chuang, I.L."}, {"full_name": "Yamamoto, Y."}]}, "raw_refs": [{"schema": "text", "value": "[32] I.L. Chuang and Y. Yamamoto, Phys. Rev. A 55, 114 (1997), LANL preprint quant-ph/9704030."}], "record": {"$ref": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/408074"}}, {"reference": {"label": "33", "publication_info": {"journal_volume": "55", "artid": "900", "year": 1997, "page_start": "900", "journal_title": "Phys.Rev.A"}, "authors": [{"full_name": "Knill, E."}, {"full_name": "Laflamme, R."}]}, "raw_refs": [{"schema": "text", "value": "[33] E. Knill and R. Laflamme, Phys. Rev. A 55, 900 (1997)."}], "record": {"$ref": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/2730469"}}, {"reference": {"label": "34", "publication_info": {"journal_volume": "93", "artid": "7062", "year": 1989, "page_start": "7062", "journal_title": "J.Phys.Chem."}, "authors": [{"full_name": "Silbey, R."}, {"full_name": "Harris, R.A."}]}, "raw_refs": [{"schema": "text", "value": "[34] R. Silbey and R.A. Harris, J. Phys. Chem. 93, 7062 (1989)."}]}, {"reference": {"label": "35", "publication_info": {"journal_volume": "106", "artid": "7036", "year": 1997, "page_start": "7036", "journal_title": "J.Chem.Phys."}, "authors": [{"full_name": "Kosloff, R."}, {"full_name": "Ratner, M.A."}, {"full_name": "Davis, W.B."}]}, "raw_refs": [{"schema": "text", "value": "[35] R. Kosloff, M.A. Ratner and W.B. Davis, J. Chem. Phys. 106, 7036 (1997)."}]}, {"reference": {"label": "36", "misc": ["An interesting example of this general first-order stability is provided by the recently analysed situation of environmentally induced superselection of energy eigenstates for a Brownian particle interacting with an adiabatically slow bath of harmonic oscillators"]}, "raw_refs": [{"schema": "text", "value": "[36] An interesting example of this general first-order stability is provided by the recently analysed situation of environmentally induced superselection of energy eigenstates for a Brownian particle interacting with an adiabatically slow bath of harmonic oscillators"}]}, {"reference": {"imprint": {"publisher": "LANL"}, "arxiv_eprint": "quant-ph/9811026", "label": "37", "misc": ["[37]", "preprint"], "authors": [{"full_name": "Paz, J.P."}, {"full_name": "Zurek, W.H."}]}, "raw_refs": [{"schema": "text", "value": "[37]. [37] J.P. Paz, W.H. Zurek, LANL preprint quant-ph/9811026 ."}], "record": {"$ref": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/479207"}}], "number_of_pages": 16, "referenced_authors_bais": ["C.H.Bennett.1", "W.G.Unruh.1", "H.F.Hofmann.1", "S.Haroche.1", "E.B.Davies.1", "K.Lendi.1", "A.J.Vanwonderen.1", "E.Knill.1", "W.H.Zurek.1", "D.Aharonov.1", "B.Schumacher.1", "G.Mahler.4", "M.Rasetti.1", "R.Landauer.1", "L.M.Duan.1", "R.Laflamme.1", "J.M.Raimond.1", "W.K.Wootters.1", "J.A.Smolin.1", "R.Jozsa.1", "P.Tombesi.2", "M.BenOr.1", "P.Zanardi.3", "K.B.Whaley.2", "G.Lindblad.1", "I.L.Chuang.1", "G.C.Guo.1", "D.Vitali.2", "Andrew.M.Steane.1", "David.Bacon.1", "D.A.Lidar.1", "J.P.Paz.1", "F.Rossi.3", "M.A.Nielsen.2", "P.Pechukas.1", "D.P.DiVincenzo.1"], "legacy_version": "20161012002132.0", "inspire_categories": [{"term": "General Physics"}, {"term": "Quantum Physics", "source": "arxiv"}], "legacy_creation_date": "1999-02-10", "preprint_date": "1999-02", "author_count": 3, "public_notes": [{"source": "arXiv", "value": "16 pages, no figures. Several changes, including a clarification of the derivation of the Lindblad equation from the operator sum representation. To appear in Phys. Rev A"}], "first_author": {"affiliations_identifiers": [{"schema": "ROR", "value": "https://ror.org/01an7q238"}], "full_name": "Bacon, D.", "last_name": "Bacon", "first_name": "D.", "recid": 1036935}, "control_number": 495120, "dois": [{"value": "10.1103/PhysRevA.60.1944"}, {"material": "publication", "source": "arXiv", "value": "10.1103/PhysRevA.60.1944"}], "earliest_date": "1999-02", "document_type": ["article"], "texkeys": ["Bacon:1999vr"], "abstracts": [{"value": "It was shown recently [D.A. Lidar et al., Phys. Rev. Lett. 81, 2594 (1998)] that within the framework of the semigroup Markovian master equation, decoherence-free (DF) subspaces exist which are stable to first order in time to a perturbation. Here this result is extended to the non-Markovian regime and generalized. In particular, it is shown that within both the semigroup and the non-Markovian operator sum representation, DF subspaces are stable to all orders in time to a symmetry-breaking perturbation. DF subspaces are thus ideal for quantum memory applications. For quantum computation, however, the stability result does not extend beyond the first order. Thus, to perform robust quantum computation in DF subspaces, they must be supplemented with quantum error correcting codes."}, {"source": "arXiv", "value": "It was shown recently [D.A. Lidar et al., Phys. Rev. Lett. 81, 2594 (1998)] that within the framework of the semigroup Markovian master equation, decoherence-free (DF) subspaces exist which are stable to first order in time to a perturbation. Here this result is extended to the non-Markovian regime and generalized. In particular, it is shown that within both the semigroup and the non-Markovian operator sum representation, DF subspaces are stable to all orders in time to a symmetry-breaking perturbation. DF subspaces are thus ideal for quantum memory applications. For quantum computation, however, the stability result does not extend beyond the first order. Thus, to perform robust quantum computation in DF subspaces, they must be supplemented with quantum error correcting codes.", "abstract_source_suggest": {"input": "arXiv"}}], "refereed": true, "primary_arxiv_category": ["quant-ph"], "external_system_identifiers": [{"schema": "ADS", "value": "1999PhRvA..60.1944B"}, {"schema": "SPIRES", "value": "SPIRES-3961842"}], "titles": [{"title": "On the robustness of decoherence free subspaces for quantum computation"}, {"source": "arXiv", "title": "On the Robustness of Decoherence-Free Subspaces for Quantum Computation"}, {"source": "arXiv", "title": "Robustness of Decoherence-Free Subspaces for Quantum Computation"}], "facet_author_name": ["1897289_K. Birgitta Whaley", "1025066_Daniel A. Lidar", "1036935_David J. Bacon"], "core": true, "_oai": {"sets": ["Literature"], "id": "oai:inspirehep.net:495120", "updated": "2026-02-06T09:42:54.544919"}, "curated": true, "journal_title_variants": ["Phys. Rev. A", "Phys.Rev.A"], "arxiv_eprints": [{"categories": ["quant-ph"], "value": "quant-ph/9902041"}]}}], "total": 1}, "links": {"self": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=doi%3A10.1103%2Fphysreva.60.1944&size=5&page=1", "bibtex": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=doi%3A10.1103%2Fphysreva.60.1944&size=5&page=1&format=bibtex", "latex-eu": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=doi%3A10.1103%2Fphysreva.60.1944&size=5&page=1&format=